        "tipo": "gramatica",
        "min": 8,
        "titulo": "Saying your profession and asking questions with ser",
        "explicacao": "Listen: «Soy profesora. ¿Eres profesor?» STEP 1: the profession comes right after «ser», with no article: «Soy médica», «Es profesor». The article only appears if there's an adjective or a further description: «Es un buen médico.» STEP 2: many professions change in the feminine: «-o» becomes «-a» («médico, médica»; «ingeniero, ingeniera»), «-or» becomes «-ora» («profesor, profesora»). Words ending in «-e» and «-ista» don't change: «estudiante», «periodista». STEP 3: a yes/no question has the same word order as the statement; what changes is the intonation, and in writing it gets «¿ ?»: «¿Eres profesor?» The «usted» form is «¿Es usted médica?» STEP 4: question words: «¿Quién es?» (who), «¿Qué es?» (what), «¿Cómo es?» (what... like). The verb comes right after the question word: «¿Quién eres tú?», «¿Cómo es Pablo?» STEP 5: short answer: «Sí, soy profesora.» «No, no soy estudiante.» STEP 6: «oficina» is a false friend: it means office; a repair shop is «taller».",
        "comparacao": "English also says «I'm a doctor» with no article for the profession before the noun, so that part matches. The real difference is the opening «¿» mark and the fact that the verb comes right after the question word with no filler words, unlike English, which sometimes needs «do»/«does» («Do you work here?» has no Spanish equivalent of «do» at all).",
